        "problem": "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\frac{36}{33})$ means the same thing as ${}_{36}{C}_{33}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=36$ and $r=33,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{36}{C}_{33} & =\\frac{36!}{(36-33)!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36!}{3!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34\\cdot \\cancel{33!}}{3!\\cdot \\cancel{33!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42\\,840}{6} \\\\  & =7\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
